 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Ray Projection Tree
-A hardware mechanism structured as a hierarchical binary tree of $n-1$ Voxel Combination Units (VCUs) that accepts $n$ voxels along a viewing ray at the leaves and produces the final pixel color at the root [S2]. Each VCU combines two input voxels into an output voxel value in 7 time units [S2]. The tree is fed in a pipelined fashion: after an initialization time of $7 \log n$ time units, the tree processes $\log n$ rays simultaneously, producing a new pixel color every 7 time units [S2]. Unlike the original beam projection mechanism, the RPT supports various projection schemes including first/last opaque, max/min voxel value, weighted summation, and compositing [S2].
+A hardware mechanism structured as a hierarchical binary tree of Voxel Combination Units (VCUs) that accepts $n$ voxels along a viewing ray at the leaves and produces the final pixel color at the root. It differs from the DSP-based approach by using a fixed-function pipelined tree structure rather than programmable DSPs. It supports various projection schemes including first/last opaque, max/min voxel value, weighted summation, and compositing, unlike the original beam projection mechanism which was limited.
+*   **Steps:**
+    1.  Feed $n$ input voxels comprising a ray into $n/2$ VCUs.
+    2.  Each VCU combines two input voxels into an output voxel value in 7 time units, producing $n/2$ results.
+    3.  Feed the $n/2$ results into the next stage of the tree (containing $n/4$ VCUs) while simultaneously feeding the next ray of $n$ voxels into the first stage.
+    4.  After an initialization time of $7 \log n$ time units, the tree processes $\log n$ rays simultaneously in a pipeline fashion, producing a new pixel color every 7 time units.
+*   **Parameters:** VCU Latency: 7 time units per combination. Initialization Time: $7 \log n$ time units.
+*   **Layout:** Hierarchical binary tree of $n-1$ VCUs.
+*   **Source:** [S2]
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Computation:** The DSP-based shading unit requires 15 clock cycles per volume element [S1].
-*   **Pipeline Latency (RPT):** Initialization time is $7 \log n$ time units; steady-state throughput is one pixel every 7 time units [S2].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   **Hardware:** VIRIM module consisting of four boards (36x39 cm² each), with four modules per crate [S1].
-*   **Performance:** For a 256x256x128 dataset, the system achieves a frame rate of 2.5 Hz [S1].
-*   **Throughput:** The bus transfers data at 240 MBytes/sec [S1]. The geometry unit is approximately 16 times faster than the DSP [S1].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Maximum flexibility for shading models via programmable DSPs; decoupled architecture allows independent optimization of geometry and shading; real-time reloadable LUTs and memories [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires specific hardware (VIRIM); inter-DSP communication is not supported in the current model, limiting certain global shading effects [S1].
-
-**When to Use:**
-*   When real-time volume rendering with complex, programmable shading models is required on dedicated hardware.
-*   When the scene data can be efficiently generated by a preceding geometry unit.
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-*   When general-purpose flexibility or software-based shading is preferred.
-*   When inter-DSP communication is necessary for the shading model.
+*   **Performance:** Estimated at 2.5 Hz for a $256 \times 256 \times 128$ dataset using one VIRIM module (four boards, 36x39 cm² each) [S1].
+*   **Complexity:** The geometry unit operates at a speed factor of ~16 relative to the DSPs, requiring careful buffering [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** High flexibility via programmable DSPs allowing any shading model implementation, provided data remains in one sample plane. Decoupled architecture allows independent optimization of geometry and shading [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires dedicated hardware (VIRIM boards). Inter-DSP communication is not supported in the current model, relying on the master CPU for any necessary coordination [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Real-time visualization of large volume datasets where custom hardware is available.
+*   **When not to use:** When flexibility for arbitrary shading models on standard GPUs is preferred, or when inter-processor communication between shading units is required.
 
 ## Background
-The architecture was described in the mid-1990s as part of the VIRIM (Volume Rendering Image Machine) project [S1, S2]. It was developed to address the performance limitations of software-based volume rendering by leveraging custom hardware units, specifically programmable DSPs for the ray-casting phase [S1]. The Ray Projection Tree variant was introduced to optimize the compositing stage using a hierarchical hardware pipeline [S2].
+The method was developed as part of the VIRIM (Volume Rendering Image Machine) project. It was described in the paper "Three Architectures for Volume Rendering" by Westermann et al. (1995) [S1]. The architecture was designed to address the performance limitations of software-based volume rendering by utilizing specialized hardware components, specifically programmable DSPs for the ray-casting phase.
 
